  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agrologistics project coordinator Kenya Flower Council</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Dependence on airfreight transport for cut flowers and other fresh produce from Kenya to the European Union, bears substantial risks, which the COVID-19 pandemic has magnified. A reduction in air traffic due to less international air travel and thus less available belly cargo, impacted Kenya&#x2019;s ability to export of fresh flowers. And for the long-term even more important, considering airfreight&#x2019;s carbon footprint, alternatives are needed.Exploring the potential of sea freight for Kenya&#x2019;s flower export is the key to the future sustainable growth of this sector in Kenya. As part of the Netherlands embassy&#x2019;s aims to contribute to this sustainable growth, several studies have been funded. As a next step, a local focal point person for sea freight of fresh flowers and other agriclultural fresh produce will be recruited and funded for two years, to be embedded at the Kenya Flower Council. This focal point serves to raise attention to the sea freight agenda with local government actors, producers, exporters/importers, and to consolidate efforts and lessons in de-bottlenecking sea freight for fresh produce and creation of a perishable hub at Kenya&#x2019;s main seaport.</narrative>
  </description>
